SM(168,{"n":"James 1.1-15","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/James 1.1-15.mp3","d":2501.9,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.36,"And uh when we get started, we're starting at the very beginning of the Epistle of St."],[28.48,"James. Um last time I gave my big rambly introduction to the general epistles, followed by my big rambly introduction to the Epistle of St."],[41.6,"James, which two rambles encompass the entire Bible study."],[46.56,"So uh we're actually going to get into the text of the epistle tonight."],[51.36,"And so, because of that, I'm not going to spend a ton of time uh rehashing again that introduction."],[58.96,"By the time people are listening. to this online at least, they'll be able to go back and listen to that as much as they like at whatever speed they like"],[67.48,"So um all we'll say for now is just real quickly, as we talked about last time, the Saint James who's writing this, because there are a bunch, because James is through a strange set of historical and linguistic circumstances, the English version of Jacob."],[85.18,"Uh so there's a bunch of them in the in the New Testament."],[89.5,"Um this is Saint James uh Adelphoteos, the brother of God is his title, which is means Christ's brother, which means uh the son of Saint Joseph the bet Betrotrothedhed. from his marriage before he was to the Theotokos"],[111.7,"He was a widower."],[113.22,"This is his son."],[114.26,"This is the same Saint James who after Christ's resurrection, he appeared to his brother Saint James and put him in charge of the church in Jerusalem."],[126.02,"Is the bishop, the overseer, of that church."],[130.31,"And so this Saint James was, as we talked about last time, martyred around 62 AD."],[137.95,"Uh, so um that means this epistle was written before that."],[143.07,"Uh, but we don't know exactly when."],[145.64,"There aren't a lot of it's as you could imagine, distinguishing between, you know, if it was written sometime in the 50s AD, distinguishing between, well, this must have been written in 56 rather than 57 because XYZ is pretty much impossible to do."],[161.62,"We don't have enough historical information and enough historical sources to do that kind of fine-tuning, but roughly somewhere in the 50s, which means this is during the period when several of St."],[174.5,"Paul's epistles that we read were also being written."],[177.62,"We're very firmly in that sort of apostolic era, the era of St."],[181.46,"Paul's missionary journeys, uh, when this is being written."],[185.14,"And presumably, even though there's not, we just call it the Epistle of St."],[190.18,"James, we don't say to anyone, because there's no one. sort of indicated"],[195.74,"We can assume that this is a letter that was written first to the church that he oversaw."],[201.56,"The community in Jerusalem, but also we know that obviously it circulated widely, and as we're going to see as we start, he likely intended for it to uh to circulate uh widely."],[215.48,"So let that suffice for recap from last time."],[219.24,"Hopefully it does suffice."],[220.92,"Umless anybody has any further questions about that, we'll go ahead and get started here in uh Epistle of St."],[228.28,"James, uh, chapter one, verse one."],[233.32,"So James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greetings."],[242.8,"So uh as we mentioned last time, that first part of verse one, the James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, is not actually in the earliest manuscripts that we have."],[255.04,"It's labeled as the Epistle of Saint James."],[257.86,"But that identifier uh isn't there."],[261.78,"So it's not that this was circulating around anonymously."],[266.12,"Because it wasn't."],[267.0,"It was labeled as the Epistle of St."],[268.84,"James. But uh just that as we talked about before, uh these texts didn't circulate individually for very long."],[280.41,"People were passing around copies of individual books of the Bible."],[281.29,"They were very quickly being collected and put together"],[287.17,"And when you put things together, especially when you put them together with other things written by other people, you need to label them."],[295.88,"So we talked about this, or we talked about this back when we were talking about the Gospels, and there are certain folks, modern scholars like Bart Urban, who will say, Oh, well, these gospels circulated anonymously, like we don't know what the names of the authors were."],[311.16,"And as other scholars have pointed out, well, if they're circulating together, all four of them, you gotta call them something."],[320.14,"Even if you just number them one, two, three, four."],[322.72,"You have to have some way of identifying them."],[327.36,"And we have zero evidence of the Gospels being called anything other than Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John."],[334.48,"So why would you think they circulated anonymously."],[337.34,"So the same kind of thing is true here with the epistle of St."],[341.1,"James. A lead-in like that gets added, just because it's being collected into a book."],[347.84,"And you're separating out."],[349.2,"And if you consider that you're right, you're doing daily readings out of this book, you're gonna want an introductory statement."],[357.45,"So this actually happens like in our gospel and epistle books, sometimes."],[362.57,"And it's kind of subtle, so a lot of people don't notice it."],[365.47,"Those are called lectionary books because they're divided into readings."],[369.4,"And so when you're just sitting here with a Bible like the Orthodox Study Bible and reading straight through a book, you don't need to introduce each section."],[380.49,"But sometimes if you take a piece, like you take a piece out of St."],[383.85,"Matthew's Gospel, and we're just gonna read this piece today."],[387.5,"Well, sometimes you need to put an introductory statement."],[392.07,"Because that piece might just be like a long quote."],[395.03,"So if you just read the long quote, you don't know who's saying it."],[399.93,"Like who they're saying it to."],[402.19,"So sometimes if it's like a chunk of math, you'll be like, it'll they'll add the Lord said or Jesus said to those who came out to him, and then they have the quote."],[413.29,"So you add a little introduction, to sort of set what's going on."],[418.39,"And the same thing happens in the epistles."],[421.78,"That's the most common example is that the word brethren gets tacked on to sometimes it's actually there, because St."],[427.3,"Paul uses that language a lot, but sometimes also it's just put there as sort of an introduction."],[433.24,"Because you're sort of starting in the middle of one of St."],[435.4,"Paul's epistles. And so you want to have something introductory."],[442.16,"And so the same kind of thing here."],[444.1,"So if we consider, you have this text, and we talked about how, for example, the fourth century, the Epistle of St."],[449.67,"James came right after the book of Acts"],[456.51,"In the pr the not printed, the written New Testaments that we have from the time."],[462.67,"So you've just finished reading Acts the day before."],[467.31,"You turn the page and start reading with to the twelve tribes."],[475.72,"That doesn't make a lot of sense."],[477.34,"So that explains why we get added."],[481.84,"James too. Segway."],[483.83,"For the readings."],[486.39,"So this isn't like this was floating around with no name attached to it, and then sometime in the late second century they decided it came from St."],[495.87,"James. This is it was circulating under the name of St."],[498.29,"James, and that particular line got added for lectionary purposes."],[502.47,"For reading purposes."],[506.95,"He identifies him as a bondservant, that's literally a slave."],[511.17,"Of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ."],[516.77,"So that to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, this is one of the most overinterpreted small groups of words in the Bible."],[528.61,"So we mentioned last time that we'd have to talk about this as we go."],[534.61,"People have taken from this. and from a certain characterization of the way St"],[541.21,"James is presented in the book of Acts and said, oh, well, see, St."],[546.33,"James is writing this just to Jewish Christians."],[551.61,"So there's been this whole construct starting with uh really starting with Martin Luther."],[560.17,"Uh and then scholars afterwards culminating in 19th century German scholars to sort of create these two different kinds of Christianity."],[570.91,"So Martin Luther wanted to read St."],[573.31,"Paul in a certain way."],[575.87,"Specifically, he wanted St."],[577.71,"Paul to be sort of condemning the law and commandments and works and all of that stuff."],[585.6,"And therefore, and so St."],[588.4,"Paul goes out to these Gentiles."],[590.72,"And so, oh, well, there's this Gentile church that St."],[594.72,"Paul starts. Where they're free from the law."],[598.86,"There's an old Lutheran hymn."],[600.22,"Free from the law, oh happy condition."],[603.02,"Yay, there's no law."],[608.62,"And then contrast that then with this quote-unquote Jewish Christianity, which is legalistic."],[616.76,"And Saint James is seen as, well, he's in Jerusalem, so he's like the figurehead of this."],[622.34,"And so you have this Jewish Christianity, and then you have this Pauline Gentile Christianity over here, and that Pauline Gentile Christianity sort of wins out for a while."],[635.48,"But so now this then, this concept, which is really just a hypothesis, without a lot of good evidence for it, then gets spun out in all kinds of ideological ways."],[649.63,"So for Martin Luther, oh, the Roman Catholic Church is like those legalistic, Jewish Christians."],[659.5,"And I'm trying to bring back St."],[661.74,"Paul's gospel. And then uh as it goes on, when you get into like 18th and 19th century Germans who just happen to be incredibly anti-Semitic, uh then it's oh we'll see this Jewish Christianity bad."],[681.44,"There's legalism and Saint Paul Christianity."],[684.16,"Oh, it's all about freedom."],[685.92,"There's no Jew or Greek male or female."],[689.26,"Forget the old testament."],[691.52,"Old Testament is Jewish and bad."],[693.78,"It gets spun out like this."],[695.3,"This gets picked up by uh modern Islamic apologists, believe it or not, who want to say Islam is the continuation of this Jewish Christianity."],[707.46,"Paul is turning it into this weird Greek thing"],[709.04,"And Paul's the bad guy."],[714.41,"And you can hear this in all kinds of Protestant apologists."],[717.75,"You know, where they're setting apart, you know, oh, the later church becomes all Greek, you know, and not Hebrew."],[724.09,"Not Hebraic. It loses its Jewish roots."],[726.33,"And then you get the Jewish roots movement in American evangelicalism."],[731.47,"And all of this is based on just about nothing."],[736.29,"We already went through Acts 15 and saw that St."],[738.93,"James was fundamentally in agreement with Saints Peter and Paul about how to handle Gentiles becoming Christians."],[749.13,"And uh while, yes, in Jerusalem, as you might expect, the majority of the Christians there were Jewish."],[749.17,"And there's no evidence of this conflict."],[761.22,"Because Jewish means Judean, and Jerusalem was the capital of Judea."],[767.62,"So guess what?"],[771.59,"That does not necessarily mean that the Christianity there was of a completely different character."],[779.16,"And so as we went through St."],[781.24,"Paul's epistles, hopefully we pretty well demolished the idea that he was anti-law or anti-Torah."],[789.22,"I mean, he in the book of Acts was constantly. having to defend himself from those accusations"],[795.9,"You know, and only to have Luther turn up and start making them again uh 1500 years later."],[802.3,"But that's right, he's always defending himself against so that whole part of it isn't true."],[807.63,"But the St."],[808.67,"James part, as we're gonna see, also isn't true."],[811.95,"So what's the problem with interpreting to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, as meaning he's writing to Jewish Christians?"],[820.51,"Anyone know off the top of their head?"],[821.95,"Or remember when we talked about Romans uh 11?"],[828.49,"So so the Jews are Judeans."],[831.9,"And Judea comes from Judah."],[833.76,"This is the tribe of Judah that returned from exile."],[834.48,"One How many tribes is that?"],[841.0,"And you've got some people from the tribe of Benjamin, like St."],[844.6,"Paul. You've got some Levites around."],[846.76,"So you got sort of two and a half tribes."],[849.24,"So if he's writing to the twelve tribes scattered abroad."],[854.7,"That means he's also writing to the ten tribes of the northern kingdom."],[858.38,"And what happened to them?"],[862.91,"The Assyrians took them and deported them, and they assimilated into the nations."],[869.49,"That was the scattering abroad."],[872.29,"That's the diaspora."],[874.15,"They guess right, they innerbred."],[875.83,"And we talked about how this is how St."],[877.67,"Paul. describes it"],[878.91,"He uses the language in Romans 11 that he gets from Genesis."],[883.97,"In Genesis 48, there's a scene where there's a scene right after that more people have read."],[894.29,"So there's a scene right after where Jacob blesses his 12 sons, like the 12 tribes."],[902.26,"And people are somewhat familiar with that, especially his prophecy to Judah, because there's connections there to Palm Sunday, and we read it the night before, and that kind of thing."],[911.46,"But before that, he blesses Joseph's two sons, Manasseh and Ephraim"],[917.3,"That doesn't get read as much by people for whatever reason."],[920.58,"And when he does it, Manasseh is the older son, and Ephraim is the younger son"],[927.9,"But as is a theme in the book of Genesis, he switches his hands, he crosses his hands."],[936.06,"So he puts his right hand on Ephraim, the younger son's head, to give him the greater blessing."],[942.41,"To give him like the firstborn of Joseph status."],[945.61,"And Ephraim is the central tribe of the northern kingdom."],[950.25,"That's where the capital is."],[952.55,"And all through the Psalms and the prophets, the northern kingdom is referred to as Ephraim."],[958.49,"When they talk about all Israel, they say Judah and Ephraim."],[961.79,"Those are the two like heads."],[964.75,"The blessing he gives to Ephraim is he says that Ephraim's descendants will become the fullness of the Gentiles."],[971.55,"Those are the words he uses."],[973.79,"So St. Paul picks up on that in Romans 11 to describe the gospel going out to the Gentiles."],[981.28,"To say, there's the Judeans, Judah, the Jews, and then the fullness of the Gentiles will come in, and then all Israel will be saved."],[995.77,"So if God is going to restore all twelve tribes, that means he's got to restore those other ten."],[1002.81,"And he promises to restore those other ten all over the place in Isaiah, Jer inemiah. and Ezekiel"],[1009.73,"If he's going to do that, and they're out there dispersed, diaspora, scattered among the nations, he's got to bring them back from the nations."],[1020.44,"So saying the twelve tribes scattered across the world is not saying to all the Jews out there, wherever you're living, it's talking about the whole church."],[1035.88,"That's a way of talking about the whole church."],[1038.76,"Because as St."],[1039.56,"Paul said, the Jews have been grafted in."],[1042.44,"And so this way of talking that he's using, by the way, it' nots opposed to the way St."],[1047.8,"Paul talks. In fact, it reflects the same. understanding"],[1053.28,"If he was writing just to Jews, he would not say to the twelve tribes."],[1059.44,"He would say to the Judeans, to the Jews, scattered throughout the world."],[1063.38,"Or the sons of Judah."],[1065.68,"Or the people of God."],[1067.12,"There's lots of other ways he could say it."],[1069.44,"So choosing to say twelve tribes means he shares this view."],[1077.14,"So by being grafted in."],[1079.59,"This is the church doesn't replace Israel."],[1082.23,"It's not the new Israel."],[1083.43,"The church is Israel."],[1086.39,"It's the remnant of Judea that's been purified, and then people from all the nations are grafted in to sort of refill the other ten tribes."],[1097.57,"Into which they were scattered like seed."],[1103.17,"So that's what he's writing."],[1104.45,"He's writing to everybody."],[1106.45,"That's why this is one of the general or Catholic epistles, like we talked about last time."],[1112.37,"It's to everybody."],[1120.05,"Which is the same word for greetings, uh, Karen, that's uh used at the beginning."],[1127.49,"This is the exact same way his letter begins in Acts 15."],[1130.93,"We mentioned there were some parallels between the letter, the text of St."],[1135.65,"James's letter in Acts 15."],[1137.77,"And this letter, this is one of them."],[1140.33,"Just that just uses the word greetings, doesn't have the sort of elaborate intro that St."],[1145.45,"Paul's epistles have."],[1149.5,"So, my brethren, count it all joy when you fall into various trials."],[1158.06,"So, we'll pause there."],[1160.16,"This is not our instinct."],[1162.3,"We don't say, Yay, I'm struggling."],[1165.74,"Yay, my life has gotten difficult."],[1168.06,"Woo! So he's saying, actually, when that happens, you should rejoice."],[1175.42,"You should have joy."],[1176.86,"Why? Verse 3. knowing that the testing of your faith produces patience"],[1178.54,"No. So that when you go through these trials."],[1187.99,"There's the testing of your faith."],[1191.51,"We're gonna talk about it again."],[1193.05,"Does he have a different view of faith than St."],[1194.97,"Paul? This is why it's important because people are gonna try and contrast for the reasons we just talked about."],[1198.73,"St. James and St."],[1199.69,"Paul. We talked about St."],[1200.97,"Paul's idea of faith is faithfulness."],[1204.05,"So is that what he means by faith?"],[1206.85,"Well, how would you test your belief that something is true in the abstract?"],[1216.73,"True false test."],[1218.19,"So, like, I believe Jesus is the Messiah, but now I'm broke."],[1225.32,"Like, why would that why would having difficulties."],[1229.53,"Now, if it's faithfulness, remaining faithful to Christ, you can understand why facing hard circumstances in life would test that."],[1238.73,"Are you going to remain faithful or are you going to turn away?"],[1244.78,"So, seems like here at the beginning of his epistle, St."],[1248.22,"James and St."],[1248.7,"Paul have pretty much the same definition."],[1250.95,"Of this word."],[1253.91,"So the testing of your faithfulness."],[1256.33,"When you go through these trials and your faithfulness to Christ is put to the test, that produces patience, long suffering."],[1263.29,"Verse 4, but let patience have its perfect work"],[1263.67,"Endurance. Great translation."],[1275.51,"We've talked about this word perfect."],[1277.61,"And it's more the idea of completion, maturity."],[1281.31,"So when your patience sort of reaches maturity, then that means, you've become more mature."],[1292.83,"This is so he's setting forth as one of the pro one of, if not the primary marker of maturity as a Christian, is having this um patience and long suffering."],[1305.47,"And contentment in the various situations in life"],[1309.71,"You could see why that would be a marker."],[1312.76,"Of someone who's mature."],[1314.06,"Like little kids are not often very content."],[1318.16,"They don't have a long attention span."],[1321.04,"Worst torture in the world is having to sit in one place and not do anything for like 30 seconds."],[1328.32,"But uh once they're able to do that, once they're able to just sit quietly with their thoughts for a few minutes at a time, you say, oh, well, they are now more mature."],[1339.46,"And a whole lot of other things, they have to acquire a whole lot of other things in order to be able to do that."],[1344.56,"So this is the same kind of thing."],[1346.85,"Being able to be patiently and contentedly live your life even when it gets very difficult is a marker that you've actually achieved a lot of different virtues and capacities in your life."],[1365.77,"Verse 5: if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God who gives to all liberally and without reproach, and it will be given to him."],[1387.61,"How is wisdom related to this?"],[1389.85,"Well, wisdom is one of those things you have to have and able to in order to be content and to endure difficulties, and trials."],[1400.87,"Because if you don't understand what's happening, and you don't understand how things work, you don't understand what God is doing when these things happen, which is a place we all are at different points."],[1414.59,"So that's why he's saying, if any of you lacks wisdom, if any of you are struggling to understand what's going on and how to endure and how to keep going."],[1422.37,"Then you need to ask God."],[1424.62,"Because God gives generously."],[1427.48,"To everybody. And without finding fault means it's not like, you know, oh, I haven't been a good person."],[1434.74,"I'm in this mess because of my sin, which is usually true."],[1438.44,"And therefore, God's not going to help me."],[1441.86,"He's saying that's not how God operates."],[1444.67,"If you ask him for the wisdom and you're trying to struggle on and endure."],[1450.05,"He will be there to aid you and help you and help you understand."],[1457.41,"Verse 6, but let him ask in faith with no doubting."],[1460.77,"For he who doubts is like a wave of the sea driven and tossed by the wind."],[1465.81,"So, once again, remember, belief and faith are the same thing."],[1471.89,"So is he saying, No, you gotta really believe that he's gonna give you wisdom, or he won't give it to you."],[1481.29,"That's what I'm saying."],[1483.39,"Or is he saying, that if you're not living your life faithfully to God, then don't bother asking."],[1497.62,"And where would he be getting that idea?"],[1499.7,"Well, read the prophets."],[1501.16,"Remember, at a certain point, the prophets say, hey, stop offering the sacrifices."],[1507.4,"God doesn't want them from you."],[1509.64,"Because you're not living faithfully."],[1511.98,"You can't just go out and live however you want and then offer the sin offerings and be like, oh, I'm okay."],[1517.34,"No, I gave the right."],[1519.34,"That's not how it works"],[1521.04,"So you need to live faithfully."],[1523.9,"And then. So if you're in a situation where your life is now a mess, and it's because of your own sin."],[1534.44,"Which is how that happens to us."],[1536.59,"Then it's not that, oh, well, ask God to bail you out and he'll bail you out."],[1542.11,"Because, like he has to."],[1544.61,"Because he doesn't."],[1546.14,"He is generous and giving."],[1547.84,"But the other side of that is you need to be seeking to remain faithful."],[1553.3,"You need to be actively struggling and striving to do better."],[1557.4,"Those two things go together."],[1560.96,"That's what we call synergy."],[1563.06,"You're working together."],[1564.42,"It's not you just keep sinning."],[1566.82,"You just keep doing what got you into the mess in the first place and expect God to bail you out."],[1574.19,"And make everything right."],[1576.27,"Yes, sir. What about the word doubt?"],[1581.55,"In English, that's like it's the opposite of what you're doing."],[1585.37,"And so that's why they translated it that way."],[1588.82,"So this is more like wavering."],[1591.08,"The idea is as opposed to being faithful."],[1594.4,"Instead of following the path, straying from the path. you're"],[1598.32,"Right? Instead of walking a straight line, you're zigzagging."],[1603.94,"And that's why he says, the person who does that, the person who wavers is like a wave of the sea that just gets blown about by the wind."],[1614.43,"Is that why a waver sounds like a wave?"],[1616.91,"Yes. But that's right, you just get blown back and forth."],[1621.73,"So whatever's whatever's happening in the world is just going to carry you along."],[1627.67,"Because you're not actively."],[1630.17,"So this is active versus passive."],[1633.63,"Um, Luther may have understood this, and that's why he didn't like it."],[1637.48,"The idea is that we aren't just sort of passive when we call on God and God does it all for us."],[1644.14,"But that we have to be actively striving."],[1647.12,"Because if we're not, then we're just gonna get right tossed around and end up wherever we wash up."],[1660.88,"Verse 7 for let not that man suppose that he will receive anything from the Lord."],[1665.52,"He is a double minded man, unstable in all his ways."],[1670.32,"So again, that kind of person, shouldn't expect God to give him any help because he's not trying to be faithful to God."],[1689.04,"Verse 9 Let the lowly brother glory in his exaltation, but the rich in his humiliation, because as the flower of the field, he will pass away."],[1700.64,"For no sooner has the sun risen with a burning heat than it withers the grass, its flower falls, and its beautiful appearance perishes."],[1709.52,"So the rich man also will fade away in his pursuits."],[1715.92,"But anyway. So notice how it starts, though."],[1723.76,"I like how they translate it the lowly brother."],[1725.92,"We don't want to say poor."],[1727.84,"Because we don't want to make this about money."],[1729.52,"But we've talked about this"],[1733.48,"This is this is how justice works."],[1735.66,"Justice is everything's in its right place, everything is functioning properly."],[1740.56,"And so when God judges, that means he's putting everything back in order."],[1746.64,"Okay. Well, if you're lowly, if you're someone who's been humiliated in this world, then when God sets things right, that's exaltation for you."],[1759.16,"You get lifted up."],[1760.45,"If the scales are rebalanced."],[1762.15,"You go up."],[1763.29,"It's saying, so you should obviously rejoice in that."],[1767.15,"God is going to lift you up when you've been humiliated."],[1770.85,"But then the reverse."],[1773.62,"The one who's up here on top in this world is going to come down."],[1779.0,"Is going to experience the humiliation."],[1781.16,"But notice St."],[1782.28,"James isn't saying, oh, rich people can all go to hell."],[1786.18,"That's not. He's saying that rich person should rejoice in their humiliation."],[1793.96,"They should be happy about it."],[1796.34,"And this is connected to what he was just talking about, the trials, the difficulties in life."],[1802.3,"That we tend to rejoice when everything is going well in our lives, materially."],[1811.39,"Jobs going' good, money's coming in, all the bills are paid, getting along with everybody."],[1817.4,"Everything's fine. That's when we rejoice."],[1820.54,"That's when we rejoice."],[1822.62,"Problem is, usually when that's happening, it's not because we're being really faithful to Christ."],[1830.6,"We're usually on cruise control because we're on the wrong road and the devil doesn't want to bother us because that might make us repent."],[1840.88,"And when something happens, and we face the trials."],[1845.38,"When we go from being blessed and being rich in blessings to being humiliated, that's not when we rejoice."],[1852.98,"That's what we cry."],[1853.86,"Why is God doing this to me?"],[1857.5,"Doesn't God care about me?"],[1859.1,"Doesn't He And St."],[1863.35,"James is saying, no, that's what you should rejoice."],[1865.27,"You should rejoice in the trials."],[1866.87,"You should rejoice in that humiliation."],[1871.01,"Because this is for your good."],[1873.43,"This is for your good."],[1874.79,"And after all, as he points out, all that stuff that made you rich, in scare quotes for people listening to the recording."],[1886.12,"All that stuff, that stuff's all going away."],[1889.88,"That stuff is here today, gone tomorrow."],[1894.97,"Uh the richest man in world history so far."],[1900.69,"It's the head of a North African trade empire in the Middle Ages."],[1904.61,"You've probably never even heard of it."],[1906.45,"I think I have."],[1910.53,"Yeah. Most people haven't even heard of him today."],[1915.67,"He once spent so much gold in Egypt in three days, he destroyed their economy for years."],[1920.71,"Literally. Yeah, it was far as like."],[1926.76,"All gone now."],[1929.73,"So, uh, that's the point."],[1932.58,"So all that stuff that you were rejoicing over, why are you rejoicing over that?"],[1937.14,"That's like rejoicing over, you know, oh, there's this flower blooming outside my house."],[1938.5,"And it's pretty"],[1945.02,"It's like, well, okay, that's nice."],[1947.92,"It's not bad."],[1949.7,"But you know what?"],[1950.74,"The sun's gonna come up."],[1952.82,"It's gonna die."],[1954.18,"And then the wind's gonna blow it away."],[1956.74,"So why get all excited about that?"],[1959.79,"That's not what's uh that's not what's important."],[1963.25,"And that's why he says that the so the rich man also will fade away in his pursuits."],[1967.57,"All that stuff that you spend your whole life chasing after, no matter how much of it you catch for a little while, is all gonna disappear."],[1979.34,"And so that's not what you should be rejoicing over."],[1980.3,"you You should be rejoicing over the difficulties that help bring closer to Christ and conform you to the likeness of Christ and help bring you to maturity in Christ."],[1991.7,"Right to become the person who God created you to be."],[2010.86,"Love him. So we talked about already that or last time we talked about that another of the parallels we see a lot of in St."],[2026.7,"James Epistle is to Christ's teachings specifically in St."],[2031.02,"Matthew's Gospel. This is one of them."],[2033.76,"When you heard that blessed is the man."],[2037.1,"That may have already caught your ear in terms of the Beatitudes in St."],[2042.86,"Matthew's Gospel. And it is using that same kind of format."],[2047.72,"That same kind of idea."],[2051.24,"So this idea of blessed, conveys um a kind of like sometimes it's translated as happiness."],[2062.51,"Happy is the man."],[2063.57,"But our our view of happiness, especially today, as modern Americans is pr,etty shallow and pathetic."],[2070.43,"Uh but this is this is more uh the idea of a sort of like heavenly bliss."],[2079.49,"This is more the idea of this is the person, it's like happiness contentment, like real, this is this is the kind of person who you should envy."],[2090.18,"And and part of what Christ is doing in the Beatitudes, is he saying, Blessed are, the people you should envy, the people you should try to be like, and then he names somebody who no one would want to be like."],[2101.63,"Blessed are those who mourn."],[2103.15,"Like, wait, what?"],[2105.49,"That's blessed are the poor."],[2107.41,"What? So it's the same kind of idea here that St."],[2112.22,"James is doing."],[2113.11,"Blessed is the man who endures temptation."],[2116.57,"Trials and temptations."],[2117.85,"Like, what? No, that I don't want to endure trials and temptations."],[2123.16,"But there's always a for."],[2124.94,"Just like when Christ does the Beatitudes."],[2126.86,"There's a reason why."],[2128.72,"And all of those are associated, when Christ does it, they're associated with the kingdom."],[2132.81,"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God."],[2135.87,"That kind of thing."],[2136.83,"So in the same way here, blessed is the man who endures temptation."],[2139.39,"For when he has been approved."],[2141.65,"Once you've come through the trial and remained faithful."],[2144.99,"That's what approved here means."],[2146.61,"You've gone through the trial, remained faithful."],[2158.46,"And this is why you look at iconography like that one of the martyrs, they're receiving crowns."],[2165.58,"Or you look at the uh ladder of divine ascent, and the saints who are reaching the top of the ladder."],[2172.56,"Christ is placing a crown upon their head."],[2175.78,"And that's the crown of victory."],[2179.59,"Like in the games, in the Roman games."],[2182.73,"The victor God."],[2184.17,"right The laurel."],[2185.64,"Well, it was a tough road getting there to be the victor of the games."],[2189.62,"Same kind of same kind of idea."],[2190.66,"of So there's this tough road, but at the end it, there's the prize."],[2195.04,"For the one who endures to the end."],[2197.52,"This is again very similar, not just in structure, to what Christ says in St."],[2202.72,"Matthew's Gospel. It's the kind of language St."],[2205.28,"Paul uses all the time in terms of running the race and the prize and victory."],[2210.08,"And it's the same kind of thing."],[2213.04,"There's not this sort of disparity, in what's being taught. let no one say when he is tempted"],[2226.0,"For God cannot be tempted by evil, nor does he himself tempt anyone."],[2230.48,"So part of the problem here is that we keep using the word tempted."],[2234.0,"And the way we think of temptation. isn't really this"],[2238.93,"Temptation for us is like, hey, here's a giant piece of chocolate cake."],[2246.13,"Don't you want it?"],[2247.66,"Now, yes, God doesn't do that to people."],[2251.6,"That is true."],[2253.04,"Um, but again, we're not just talking about that."],[2258.18,"We're talking about these trials."],[2260.18,"We're talking about difficulties in life, we're talking about hardships in life."],[2264.76,"Which goes beyond just the idea of someone trying to talk you into doing something bad."],[2272.25,"So he's saying, no one let no one say this."],[2274.57,"I'm being tempted by God."],[2275.77,"Meaning, when you're going through difficulties, why is God doing this to me?"],[2275.93,"ing. God is punish me"],[2282.42,"God is making my life difficult."],[2287.51,"Because God is not, God is not tested by evil."],[2291.61,"God doesn't have a bad day."],[2293.79,"There's nothing that can sort of impose circumstances on God."],[2297.39,"Nor does he himself tempt anyone."],[2299.65,"He's not in the business of ruining people's lives."],[2303.1,"That's not what God is about."],[2305.58,"Verse 14, but each one is tempted when he is drawn away by his own desires and enticed."],[2311.44,"When we're facing hard times in life, it's our own doing."],[2316.64,"That's how we get into a mess"],[2319.94,"We're not cruising along and we're doing everything right and we're innocent, and then God comes and wrecks our party."],[2329.11,"It's that we through our sinfulness and the sinfulness of other people."],[2333.77,"And the interaction of that, we end up in these difficulties and these trials."],[2338.25,"And God is not in the business of doing these bad things to us."],[2342.62,"He's in the business of helping us endure and get through them."],[2345.9,"As St. James already said."],[2347.68,"With the wisdom comment."],[2349.94,"God is there to work with us and help us through them."],[2361.44,"Well, it's not, it's not a one-to-one correlation."],[2364.9,"But it's still a result of sin in the world."],[2368.26,"Yeah. It's a broken world."],[2370.91,"Yes, yeah, yeah."],[2372.51,"It's not that God causes you to be in a car accident."],[2377.25,"Like that's that's what he's trying to break down."],[2380.39,"This direct correlation that God is doing this to me."],[2383.13,"And why would he say that here?"],[2384.65,"Well, he just said, hey, you should be happy when you go through these hard times because this is gonna do these positive things for you."],[2392.73,"But now he wants to say, no, don't."],[2394.73,"I don't mean that God is doing these things to you in order to, you know, what doesn't kill you makes you stronger."],[2402.05,"So God's gonna torture you to make you stronger or something."],[2405.11,"That's not what he's saying."],[2406.31,"Sorry, Calvinist. Um that God isn't behind directly behind everything."],[2411.51,"There's sin in the world."],[2413.03,"That's what causes these things."],[2415.68,"If there wasn't sin in the world, if we were still living in the world as God created it, where he called everything good, then these kind of things wouldn't happen."],[2426.43,"But it's our sin that produces these things, and it's God who then brings good out of our evil."],[2434.77,"Not that God causes evil."],[2439.89,"Which is an important distinction."],[2441.97,"Verse 15 Then when desire has conceived, it gives birth to sin."],[2452.41,"And sin when it is full grown, brings forth death."],[2457.29,"So this is this is sort of the pattern."],[2460.65,"We have something we desire, we have something we want."],[2469.61,"And then we enter into sin."],[2472.91,"And if we keep going down that path of sin, the end of that path is death."],[2479.47,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2486.87,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2492.15,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.28,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: St. James the brother of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting at the <b>very beginning</b> of the Epistle of <b>St. James</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last time: \"big rambly introduction\" to the <b>general epistles</b> + to <b>James</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two rambles = the entire Bible study</li><li class=\"d1\">tonight: <b>into the text</b>; listeners can go back to the intro online</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>James</b> = English version of <b>Jacob</b> - strange historical and linguistic circumstances</li><li class=\"d1\">a bunch of them in the NT</li><li class=\"d0\">This one: <b>St. James <i>Adelphos</i></b> - \"brother of God\" = <b>Christ's brother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">son of <b>St. Joseph the Betrothed</b> from his marriage <b>before</b> the Theotokos - Joseph a <b>widower</b></li><li class=\"d1\">after the resurrection Christ appeared to him, put him in charge as <b>bishop</b> (overseer) of <b>Jerusalem</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Martyred ~62 AD</b> → epistle written <b>before</b> that; exact date unknown</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely meant to <b>circulate widely</b></li></ul>"},{"t":228.28,"lv":2,"h":"1:1 - James, a bondservant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Text: \"James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the <b>twelve tribes</b> which are scattered abroad, greetings\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First part (\"James, a bondservant…\") <b>not in the earliest manuscripts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">labeled <b>Epistle of St. James</b> - so <b>not anonymous</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Texts didn't circulate individually for long → <b>collected and put together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">collected with other authors' writings → you <b>need to label</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">Gospels: modern scholars like <b>Bart Ehrman</b> - \"circulated anonymously\"</li><li class=\"d1\">reply: circulating together, all four → <b>you gotta call them something</b> - even number them 1-4</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>zero evidence</b> of any name but Matthew, Mark, Luke, John</li><li class=\"d0\">Same here: lead-in added <b>because it's collected into a book</b></li></ul>"},{"t":357.45,"lv":3,"h":"Lectionary lead-ins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Happens in our <b>gospel and epistle books</b> - \"kind of subtle\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>lectionary</b> books = divided into <b>readings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reading straight through (Orthodox Study Bible) → no intro needed</li><li class=\"d0\">A piece of Matthew = might be a <b>long quote</b> → who's saying it, to whom?</li><li class=\"d1\">so they add \"<b>the Lord said</b>…\" / \"Jesus said to those who came out to him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistles: most common example = <b>\"brethren\"</b> tacked on</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes really there - Paul uses it a lot; sometimes just an <b>introduction</b></li><li class=\"d0\">4th c.: James came <b>right after Acts</b> in written NT</li><li class=\"d1\">finish Acts, turn page, start with \"<b>twelve tribes</b>\" → doesn't make sense = the <b>segue</b></li><li class=\"d0\">NOT floating nameless till late 2nd c. - always under <b>St. James's</b> name; line added for <b>lectionary purposes</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Doulos</i> = literally a <b>slave</b> of God</li></ul>"},{"t":516.77,"lv":2,"h":"\"The twelve tribes scattered abroad\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"One of the most <b>overinterpreted</b> small groups of words in the Bible\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Claim: from how James appears in <b>Acts</b> → he writes <b>just to Jewish Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Whole construct starts with <b>Martin Luther</b>, culminates in <b>19th c. German scholars</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two kinds of Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">Luther wanted <b>Paul</b> condemning <b>law, commandments, works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ Gentile church \"<b>free from the law</b>\" - old Lutheran hymn: \"free from the law, oh happy condition\"</li><li class=\"d1\">vs. \"Jewish Christianity\" = <b>legalistic</b>, James in Jerusalem as figurehead</li><li class=\"d1\">Pauline Gentile Christianity \"wins out for a while\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Really just a <b>hypothesis</b>, without a lot of good evidence\" - then spun out ideologically</li></ul>"},{"t":649.63,"lv":3,"h":"How the hypothesis got spun","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Luther</b>: Roman Catholic Church = the legalistic Jewish Christians; he restores <b>Paul's gospel</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>18th-19th c. Germans</b> - \"incredibly <b>anti-Semitic</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish Christianity = <b>bad</b>, legalism</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul = <b>freedom</b>, \"no Jew or Greek\" → \"forget the Old Testament, Jewish and bad\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Islamic apologists</b>, believe it or not</li><li class=\"d1\">Islam = continuation of Jewish Christianity; <b>Paul = bad guy</b> making it \"weird Greek thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Protestant apologists</b>: later church became \"all Greek, not Hebraic\", lost its Jewish roots</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>Jewish roots movement</b> in American evangelicalism</li><li class=\"d0\">\"All of this is based on just about <b>nothing</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":736.29,"lv":3,"h":"No evidence of a conflict","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b>: James <b>in agreement</b> with Saints <b>Peter and Paul</b> on handling Gentiles</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>No evidence</b> of this conflict</li><li class=\"d0\">Jerusalem majority were Jewish - as expected</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jewish = Judean</b>; Jerusalem = capital of Judea</li><li class=\"d1\">\"guess what?\" → doesn't mean Christianity there was a <b>different character</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's epistles: \"pretty well <b>demolished</b>\" idea he was <b>anti-law / anti-Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in Acts constantly defending himself against those accusations</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>Luther</b> turns up <b>1500 years later</b> and makes them again</li><li class=\"d0\">\"But the St. James part, as we're gonna see, also <b>isn't true</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":811.95,"lv":2,"h":"Why twelve tribes isn't just Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: problem with reading it as writing to <b>Jewish Christians</b>? - recall <b>Romans 11</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jews = Judeans</b> → from <b>Judah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tribe of Judah returned from exile = <b>one tribe</b></li><li class=\"d1\">+ some <b>Benjamin</b> (like Paul), some <b>Levites</b> = \"two and a half tribes\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Writing to <b>twelve</b> tribes → includes the <b>ten tribes of the northern kingdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Assyrians</b> took and deported them → assimilated into the nations</li><li class=\"d1\">= the <b>scattering abroad</b>, the <b>diaspora</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"interbred\"</li></ul>"},{"t":883.97,"lv":3,"h":"Genesis 48: Ephraim and Manasseh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul's language in <b>Romans 11</b> comes from <b>Genesis</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gen 48</b>: right before Jacob blesses his <b>twelve sons</b> (Judah prophecy → Palm Sunday connections)</li><li class=\"d1\">first blesses <b>Joseph's sons</b>, <b>Manasseh and Ephraim</b> - not read as much</li><li class=\"d0\">Manasseh = older, Ephraim = younger → <b>crosses his hands</b> (theme in Genesis)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>right hand on Ephraim</b> = greater blessing, \"firstborn of Joseph status\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ephraim</b> = central tribe of northern kingdom, capital there</li><li class=\"d1\">Psalms and prophets: northern kingdom = \"Ephraim\"; all Israel = \"<b>Judah and Ephraim</b>\" - the two heads</li><li class=\"d0\">Blessing: Ephraim's descendants become \"<b>the fullness of the Gentiles</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul picks it up in Romans 11: Judah, then <b>fullness of Gentiles</b> comes in, then <b>all Israel saved</b></li></ul>"},{"t":995.77,"lv":3,"h":"Restoring all twelve = the whole church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God to restore all twelve → must restore the <b>other ten</b></li><li class=\"d1\">promised all over <b>Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">dispersed among nations → must <b>bring them back</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So \"twelve tribes scattered\" ≠ all the Jews wherever they live → <b>the whole church</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: Jews <b>grafted in</b> - James's way of talking reflects the <b>same understanding</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if just Jews: \"to the <b>Judeans</b>\", \"sons of Judah\", \"the people of God\"</li><li class=\"d1\">choosing <b>twelve tribes</b> = shares this view</li><li class=\"d0\">Church doesn't replace Israel - \"not the <i>new</i> Israel\" - <b>the church is Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>remnant of Judea</b> purified + nations grafted in to <b>refill the other ten tribes</b></li><li class=\"d1\">scattered \"<b>like seed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Writing to <b>everybody</b> → why it's a <b>general / Catholic epistle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greeting = <i>chairein</i>, same as <b>Acts 15</b> letter - plain, no elaborate Pauline intro</li></ul>"},{"t":1149.5,"lv":2,"h":"1:2 - Count it all joy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"My brethren, count it all <b>joy</b> when you fall into various <b>trials</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not our instinct: \"Yay, I'm struggling. Woo!\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He says: when that happens → <b>rejoice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Why? → verse 3</li></ul>"},{"t":1176.86,"lv":2,"h":"1:3 - Faith as faithfulness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"The <b>testing of your faith</b> produces <b>patience</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Different view of faith than Paul? Matters because people <b>contrast James and Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's idea = <b>faithfulness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How test belief that something's true <b>in the abstract</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I believe Jesus is the Messiah, but now I'm <b>broke</b>\" - why would that test it?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faithfulness</b> to Christ → hard circumstances <b>do</b> test it</li><li class=\"d1\">remain faithful or turn away?</li><li class=\"d0\">So James and Paul have <b>pretty much the same definition</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Testing of faithfulness → <b>patience</b> = long suffering, <b>endurance</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1263.67,"lv":2,"h":"1:4 - Perfect and complete","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Let patience have its <b>perfect work</b>\" - \"great translation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Perfect\" = <b>completion, maturity</b> - \"lacking nothing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Patience and long suffering = \"one of, if not <b>the primary marker</b>\" of Christian maturity</li><li class=\"d1\">plus <b>contentment</b> in various situations of life</li><li class=\"d0\">Kids: not content, short attention span</li><li class=\"d1\">\"worst torture in the world\" = sitting in one place <b>30 seconds</b></li><li class=\"d1\">able to sit quietly with thoughts → <b>more mature</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same with living patiently and contentedly when life gets <b>very difficult</b></li><li class=\"d1\">marker you've achieved many <b>virtues and capacities</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1365.77,"lv":2,"h":"1:5 - Asking God for wisdom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"If any of you lacks <b>wisdom</b>, let him ask of God, who gives to all <b>liberally</b> and <b>without reproach</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Wisdom = one of the <b>capacities</b> needed to be content and endure trials</li><li class=\"d1\">don't understand what's happening, how things work, <b>what God is doing</b> - \"a place we all are at different points\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Struggling to understand and keep going → <b>ask God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Gives <b>generously to everybody</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"without finding fault\" = not \"I'm in this mess because of my <b>sin</b>\" (usually true) → so God won't help</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not how God operates</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ask while struggling on and enduring → he'll <b>aid you</b> and help you understand</li></ul>"},{"t":1457.41,"lv":2,"h":"1:6 - Ask in faith, no doubting","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Let him ask in faith with no doubting. He who doubts is like a <b>wave of the sea</b> driven and tossed by the wind\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Belief and faith = <b>same thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Is he saying: <b>really believe</b> or he won't give it? Or: not living <b>faithfully</b> → don't bother asking?</li><li class=\"d1\">source = the <b>prophets</b>: \"stop offering the sacrifices\", God doesn't want them - you're <b>not living faithfully</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't live however you want, offer sin offerings, \"I'm okay\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Life a mess from <b>your own sin</b> → not \"ask God to bail you out\" - <b>he doesn't have to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>is</b> generous and giving</li><li class=\"d1\">other side: seek to <b>remain faithful</b>, <b>actively struggling</b> to do better</li><li class=\"d0\">Two together = <b>synergy</b> - working together</li><li class=\"d1\">not: keep doing what got you in the mess and expect <b>God to bail you out</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1576.27,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: what about \"doubt\"?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: the word <b>doubt</b>? English = opposite of what you're doing</li><li class=\"d0\">That's why they translated it that way</li><li class=\"d1\">more like <b>wavering</b> - opposite of being faithful</li><li class=\"d1\">straying from the path; instead of a straight line, <b>zigzagging</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Wavers → \"like a <b>wave</b>\" blown about by wind</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: why does waver sound like wave? A: yes</li><li class=\"d0\">Whatever's happening in the world <b>carries you along</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Active vs. passive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>Luther</b> may have understood this, and that's why he didn't like it\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we're not passive - call on God and God does it all</li><li class=\"d1\">must be <b>actively striving</b> or \"end up wherever we <b>wash up</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1660.88,"lv":3,"h":"1:7-8 - Double-minded","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Let not that man suppose he will receive anything from the Lord. He is a <b>double-minded</b> man, <b>unstable</b> in all his ways\"</li><li class=\"d0\">That kind of person shouldn't expect God's help</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not trying to be faithful</b> to God</li></ul>"},{"t":1689.04,"lv":2,"h":"1:9-11 - The lowly and the rich","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Let the <b>lowly brother</b> glory in his <b>exaltation</b>, but the rich in his <b>humiliation</b>… as the <b>flower of the field</b> he will pass away… sun risen with burning heat… withers the grass\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not one we read a lot in America\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Lowly\" - not \"poor\" → <b>don't make it about money</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Justice</b> = everything in its <b>right place</b>, functioning properly</li><li class=\"d1\">God judges = <b>puts everything back in order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">lowly, humiliated → God sets things right = <b>exaltation</b>, lifted up; scales rebalanced</li><li class=\"d0\">The one on top comes down → <b>humiliation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">James not saying \"rich people can all go to hell\"</li><li class=\"d1\">rich should <b>rejoice in their humiliation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connected to the <b>trials</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1796.34,"lv":2,"h":"Rejoicing in trials","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We rejoice when <b>everything's going well</b> materially</li><li class=\"d1\">job good, money coming in, bills paid, getting along with everybody</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: usually not because we're <b>faithful to Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"on <b>cruise control</b> because we're on the <b>wrong road</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">devil doesn't bother us - might make us <b>repent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Go from blessed to humiliated → we <b>cry</b>: \"Why is God doing this to me? Doesn't he care?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">James: <b>rejoice in the trials</b> - \"for your good\"</li><li class=\"d0\">All that made you rich (\"scare quotes\") = <b>gone tomorrow</b></li><li class=\"d1\">richest man in history = head of a <b>North African trade empire</b>, Middle Ages - most haven't heard of him</li><li class=\"d1\">spent so much <b>gold in Egypt in three days</b> he destroyed their economy for years</li><li class=\"d1\">\"All gone now\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1932.58,"lv":3,"h":"The flower and what to rejoice in","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why rejoice over that? Like a <b>flower blooming</b> outside your house</li><li class=\"d1\">pretty, \"not bad\" - but sun comes up, it <b>dies</b>, wind blows it away</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not what's important\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Rich man also will <b>fade away in his pursuits</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">everything you chase, however much you catch, <b>disappears</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Rejoice instead in <b>difficulties</b> that bring you closer to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>conform you to the likeness of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">bring you to <b>maturity</b> in Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">become the person <b>God created you to be</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1991.7,"lv":2,"h":"1:12 - Blessed is the man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Blessed</b> is the man who endures <b>temptation</b>. When approved, he will receive the <b>crown of life</b>, which the Lord promised to those who love him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Another <b>parallel</b> with Christ's teaching, esp. <b>Matthew</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Blessed is the man\" → the <b>Beatitudes</b> - same format</li><li class=\"d0\">Sometimes \"happy\" - our view of happiness \"<b>shallow and pathetic</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">more like <b>heavenly bliss</b>, real contentment</li><li class=\"d1\">= the person you should <b>envy</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ names someone nobody wants to be: \"blessed are those who <b>mourn</b>\" - \"What?\" - \"the <b>poor</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same here: endure <b>trials and temptations</b> - \"I don't want to\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But always a <b>\"for\"</b> - a reason; Christ's all tied to the <b>kingdom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"pure in heart, for they shall <b>see God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Approved</b> = went through the trial, <b>remained faithful</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2158.46,"lv":3,"h":"The crown","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same <b>eschatological kingdom</b> language</li><li class=\"d0\">Iconography: <b>martyrs receiving crowns</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ladder of Divine Ascent</b> - Christ places a crown on saints reaching the top</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Crown of victory</b> - Roman games, the <b>laurel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">tough road to be victor → at the end, the <b>prize</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for the one who <b>endures to the end</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Similar to Matthew <b>and</b> Paul: <b>running the race</b>, prize, victory</li><li class=\"d0\">No <b>disparity</b> in what's taught</li></ul>"},{"t":2226.0,"lv":2,"h":"1:13 - God doesn't tempt","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Let no one say when he is tempted, 'I am tempted by God.' God <b>cannot be tempted by evil</b>, nor does he himself <b>tempt</b> anyone\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: we keep using \"<b>tempted</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">for us = \"here's a giant piece of <b>chocolate cake</b>, don't you want it?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God doesn't do that - true, but <b>not just that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Talking about <b>trials, difficulties, hardships</b> - beyond someone talking you into something bad</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I'm tempted by God\" = \"Why is God doing this to me? God is <b>punishing</b> me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God isn't tested by evil: \"<b>doesn't have a bad day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">nothing can <b>impose circumstances</b> on God</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"in the business of <b>ruining people's lives</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2305.58,"lv":2,"h":"1:14 - Drawn away by desires","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Each one is tempted when he is <b>drawn away by his own desires</b> and enticed\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hard times = <b>our own doing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not cruising along innocent and \"God comes and <b>wrecks our party</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">our <b>sinfulness</b> + <b>others'</b> sinfulness + their interaction → trials</li><li class=\"d0\">God = in the business of <b>helping us endure</b> and get through</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>wisdom</b> comment - God works <b>with</b> us</li></ul>"},{"t":2361.44,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: sin in the world","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>one-to-one</b> correlation - still a result of <b>sin in the world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not that God causes you to be in a <b>car accident</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he's breaking down the <b>direct correlation</b>: \"God is doing this to me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why here? Just said <b>be happy in hard times</b> for the positives</li><li class=\"d1\">not: God does it to you - \"what doesn't kill you makes you stronger\", torture to strengthen</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Sorry, <b>Calvinist</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God isn't <b>directly behind everything</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sin</b> in the world causes these things</li><li class=\"d1\">world as created, everything \"good\" → they wouldn't happen</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God brings good out of our evil</b> - not that God <b>causes</b> evil</li><li class=\"d1\">\"an important distinction\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2441.97,"lv":2,"h":"1:15 - Desire, sin, death","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"When <b>desire has conceived</b>, it gives <b>birth to sin</b>. And sin when it is full grown brings forth <b>death</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>pattern</b></li><li class=\"d1\">something we <b>desire</b>, want</li><li class=\"d1\">let it <b>gestate</b></li><li class=\"d1\">enter into <b>sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keep going down that path → end = <b>death</b></li></ul>"}]});
